1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:02,390
Draw a chord across this circle at random.

2
00:00:02,770 --> 00:00:06,410
What's the chance it comes out longer than a side of this triangle?

3
00:00:06,790 --> 00:00:07,760
Pick a number.

4
00:00:09,533 --> 00:00:16,433
Joseph Bertrand published this puzzle in eighteen eighty-nine, as a warning about two words: at random.

5
00:00:16,813 --> 00:00:23,173
Leave them vague, and one college can report an average class of forty, or a hundred forty-seven.

6
00:00:23,433 --> 00:00:25,433
Start with the obvious simulation.

7
00:00:25,813 --> 00:00:28,773
Pick two points on the rim at random, and join them.

8
00:00:29,153 --> 00:00:32,673
If the chord beats the triangle's side, it turns gold.

9
00:00:32,933 --> 00:00:37,403
Run ten thousand chords, and the gold share settles near point three three.

10
00:00:38,100 --> 00:00:39,440
Geometry agrees.

11
00:00:39,820 --> 00:00:42,370
Put a corner of the triangle on the first point.

12
00:00:42,750 --> 00:00:48,010
The chord is long only when the second point lands on this far arc, a third of the rim.

13
00:00:49,266 --> 00:00:52,196
Case closed, until you code it a second way.

14
00:00:52,576 --> 00:00:59,246
Pick a random radius, a random point along it, and draw the chord through that point at right angles.

15
00:00:59,533 --> 00:01:01,543
This one settles near point five.

16
00:01:01,833 --> 00:01:07,433
A third program picks a random point anywhere inside, and makes it the chord's midpoint.

17
00:01:07,813 --> 00:01:09,813
That settles near point two five.

18
00:01:10,100 --> 00:01:14,700
Bertrand printed all three, and wrote that none is wrong, and none is right.

19
00:01:15,080 --> 00:01:17,860
Each tally is good to about one percentage point.

20
00:01:18,240 --> 00:01:23,310
They disagree by twenty-five percentage points, and none of the code has a bug.

21
00:01:23,566 --> 00:01:26,516
One point sets a chord's length: its midpoint.

22
00:01:26,896 --> 00:01:29,146
Slide it outward and the chord shrinks.

23
00:01:29,526 --> 00:01:36,996
It matches the triangle's side when the midpoint is half a radius out, on the circle that fits inside the triangle.

24
00:01:37,266 --> 00:01:45,806
So a chord is long exactly when its midpoint lands in this gold circle, and each program is just a way of scattering midpoints.

25
00:01:46,066 --> 00:01:48,226
Shrink every chord to its midpoint.

26
00:01:48,606 --> 00:01:55,616
The midpoint program spreads them evenly, so this circle, with a quarter of the area, catches a quarter of them.

27
00:01:55,900 --> 00:01:59,630
The radius program spreads them evenly along the radius.

28
00:02:00,010 --> 00:02:01,270
Where will they crowd?

29
00:02:02,570 --> 00:02:07,560
Toward the center: every ring gets an equal share, and inner rings are small.

30
00:02:07,940 --> 00:02:09,950
Half the radius, so one half.

31
00:02:10,233 --> 00:02:16,973
And the endpoints program piles them against the rim, so only a third land inside, the third we found.

32
00:02:17,233 --> 00:02:21,993
Three programs, three things spread evenly: rim, radius, area.

33
00:02:22,373 --> 00:02:24,353
At random never said which.

34
00:02:25,433 --> 00:02:37,523
Edwin Jaynes argued that a physical process picks one: straws tossed from a standing height can't be aimed, so the answer can't depend on where the circle sits or how big it is.

35
00:02:37,800 --> 00:02:46,570
One rain lands on both circles at once, and each judges with its own gold circle, so a fair recipe must score the same on both.

36
00:02:46,950 --> 00:02:49,900
Midpoint gives a quarter, then point four one.

37
00:02:50,700 --> 00:02:53,640
Endpoints give a third, then point four seven.

38
00:02:54,440 --> 00:02:57,240
Only the radius program gives a half on both.

39
00:02:58,100 --> 00:03:08,680
Jaynes and Charles Tyler tossed real broom straws at a five-inch circle, and the hundred and twenty-eight that crossed it matched the radius program's flat spread.

40
00:03:09,733 --> 00:03:11,853
Surveys hide the same two words.

41
00:03:12,233 --> 00:03:15,003
Take classes of ten, ten and a hundred.

42
00:03:15,383 --> 00:03:18,783
Pick a class at random, and the average size is forty.

43
00:03:19,163 --> 00:03:25,473
Pick a student at random, spread evenly over students instead of classes, and it's eighty-five.

44
00:03:25,853 --> 00:03:31,943
At Stony Brook in nineteen seventy-four, those two picks gave forty, and a hundred forty-seven.

45
00:03:32,700 --> 00:03:36,820
So whatever number you picked, some way of drawing chords makes it right.

46
00:03:37,200 --> 00:03:39,210
Name the way, and only one is.

47
00:03:39,590 --> 00:03:44,220
Forty and a hundred forty-seven are both right, once you say who's picked.

